remember a time I've gone this  long without being in the sky Brian  Kelly is better known as the points guy  as founder of the travel website he  warns this shock to the system will lead  to immediate and permanent changes to  the way we travel should you expect that  when you check into a hotel or get on a  plane that someone's going to be taking  your temperature health checks are here  to stay just like after 9/11 lengthy  airport checks were implemented the same  will be happening and is happening in  the airline space right now Emirates is  actually doing instant ko bid tests and  Etihad has temperature checks on their  check-in kiosks and those are just a  couple of the ways that the travel  experience will be changed forever  Air Canada will be the first airline in  North America to do temperature checks  on every passenger not just to stop  travelers from infecting each other but  because the public is nervous very  nervous about getting back out there I  hope they don't think that things are  going to be exactly as they were prior  to Kovan 19 Laurie Pennington gray is a  professor in the department of tourism  at the University of Florida the school  publishes a travel anxiety index it  shows fear and worry about travel have  increased a whopping 311 percent from  pre coronavirus days do people even want  to travel right now the majority of the  research is saying that they're still  you know some pent up demand but that  they're gonna wait six months or so  before they start to think about their  travel and a lot of that has to do with  this understanding of what the industry  is going to do to keep them safe  long before kovat 19 supermodel Naomi  Campbell documented her own airplane  disinfection routine back then it seemed  over-the-top I do not care  nobody's laughing now at American  Airlines caring for you is our priority  airlines have come to realize that deep  cleaning is a must so are masks for  flight attendants and passengers a  company called germ Falcon is developing  equipment to disinfect the entire cabin  between flights using ultraviolet light  major hotel chains are partnering with  name-brand disinfectant manufacturers  expect big changes when cruise ships  finally do return to the sea and so the  research also suggests that more than  60% expects that the industry is going  to have to provide you know PPE for the  visitors at touch points throughout the  travel experience and that there's more  than 70% that say that there's an  expectation that they're going to share  what they're doing with the visitor as  far as keeping that visitor safe there's  one basic problem the industry can't  overcome social distancing it's hard to  do in a hotel lobby or on board a cruise  ship and virtually impossible on a plane  train or bus that hasn't stopped the  rush to return to the high seas Carnival  Cruises aims to sail again in August  they've seen a remarkable surgeon  bookings for those first few trips  mostly from young people who in fact are  eager to travel of course the pandemic  may change those plans there's still a  chance those first sailings could be  called off I think the biggest  calculation is going to have to be how  many people am I going to to be around  and expose myself to and be exposed by  them in in in in various settings and  and what are the current recommendations  and and why  I one of my comfortable whiz some  airlines have tried to space out  passengers by leaving middle seats open  but they can't do that forever empty  seats don't make money and the airlines  are in dire straits Delta is losing 60  million dollars a day  Air Canada lost more than 1 billion  dollars in the first quarter carriers  are sending hundreds of older planes to  the scrapyard anticipating demand will  take years to return to what it was  most have said they'll emerge smaller  some likely won't survive even with  government bailouts  unfortunately the industry will  consolidate the weak airlines especially  budget airlines will not have the funds  to compete with the majors so the  airline industry has never seen a  challenge like this and they've got a  long road to recovery since business  travel and leisure travel are both off a  cliff those problems are cascading  through an industry that's responsible  for one in 10 jobs worldwide it is  across the board because their  connectivity and the dependency for all  these industries within this sector is  significant what we call the domino  effect in in this case like if one  airline is being impacted then you have  of course a travel agency the tour  operators their hotels no one is  expecting a quick rebound in the short  term there aren't many places to travel  to not with borders closed and not with  many countries implementing mandatory  quarantine on arrival nobody is going to  spend two weeks vacation enforced  isolation  longer-term businesses may decide travel  is just not worth the risk and when it  comes time to relax more of us may opt  to stick close to home 2020 may be the  year of the road trip back in BC the  Grubbs future vacation plans definitely  look different no flights and no cruises  not now and probably not anytime soon  unless we can see a vaccine that's going  to work  cleaning the stepped-up on the ships  we wouldn't be touring which is not  willing to take that risk we wouldn't be  going we've made the decision we're not  going on a cruise period that's end the  story too risky to ski  [Music]

